 "instruction": "Two security fixes in `sanctuary/api/`.\n\n1. `/status` and `/metrics` are unauthenticated on both HTTP servers, leaking internal state to any client that can reach the port. `/health` stays open (liveness).\n   - In `ws_server.py`, add `SanctuaryWebServer._status_request_authorized(request) -> bool`.\n     - Allow a loopback peer (`request.remote`), or a token with the `view_status` capability (`CAP_VIEW_STATUS`).\n     - The token comes from `Authorization: Bearer <token>` or a `token` query parameter, and is looked up in `self._tokens`. With no tokens configured, only loopback is allowed.\n     - `_handle_status` and `_handle_metrics` return 401 `{\"error\": \"unauthorized\"}` to anyone else.\n   - In `health.py`, which has no token system, add a module function `_peer_is_loopback(writer) -> bool`, using the writer's `peername`. Missing or malformed peer info counts as not loopback. Serve `/status` and `/metrics` only to loopback peers, and return 403 `{\"error\": \"forbidden\"}` to anyone else.\n2. Add DoS caps to the WebSocket server in `ws_server.py`, as module constants:\n   - `MAX_WS_CLIENTS = 64` and `MAX_WORLD_CLIENTS = 16`. A new `/ws` or `/ws/world` connection past the cap gets an error message containing \"capacity\", and is closed with code `WS_CLOSE_CODE_TRY_LATER = 1013` before it's registered.\n   - `MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = 100_000`. A user `message` longer than that is rejected with an error message containing \"too long\". Visitor-chat percept content is truncated to that length.",
